-- 127_misha_data.sql — load misha's extracted data (8 tables).
--
-- The raw export used `COPY ... FROM stdin`, which the node `pg` driver cannot
-- execute through client.query() — it needs the pg-copy-streams protocol, and
-- this project does not depend on it. So every COPY block was converted to
-- batched INSERTs (200 rows per statement), decoding COPY's text-format
-- escaping as documented: \N -> NULL, \n -> newline, \\ -> backslash.
-- (The source only ever uses \N and \n; no literal backslashes occur.)
--
-- Row counts below match misha's README exactly:
--   deal_extracts 3926 | replies 1169 | requirements 1347
--   requirement_theme_map 1328 | question_theme_map 596
--   phrases 50 | questions 50 | requirement_offer 0
--
-- Insert order respects the one FK in the export
-- (replies.customer_requirement_id -> requirements.id): requirements loads
-- before replies. The FK itself is added in 128, after all data is present.
--
-- These are plain INSERTs with no ON CONFLICT clause. That is deliberate: the
-- PKs and unique constraints are not created until 128, so there is no arbiter
-- for ON CONFLICT to infer and the clause would be a syntax error here.
-- Idempotency comes from the migration runner instead — it wraps each file in a
-- transaction and records the filename in schema_migrations only on COMMIT, so
-- a failure rolls the whole load back and a success is never repeated.
--
-- If you ever load this file by hand OUTSIDE the runner, run it inside an
-- explicit BEGIN/COMMIT, or a partial load will leave duplicate rows behind.


