('520', 'IFcSg4YvuVlx56msjbdf', 'WtSW7eYj4AlwWAZFwDPG', NULL, '303', 'Hi Alex,

Thank you for forwarding this over.

As per our call, having reviewed the comparison quotation, there are a few key differences that I have picked up which will have a significant impact on both the cost and overall specification. I think it is worth clarifying these points to ensure we are making a like for like comparison.

Firstly, it appears that they have allowed for standard aluminium mullions between the fixed glazing sections. For example, on W101, the central division would be formed using an aluminium mullion approximately 60–65mm wide.

￼
In contrast, our quotation allows for structural glazing within all of the Cortizo fixed frames. This means the glazing panes are joined using structural silicone glass-to-glass joints rather than aluminium mullions, creating a much cleaner and more premium aesthetic. Whilst this only represents a single joint on W101, the difference becomes much more significant on larger elements such as W102, where there are five vertical joints across a wide expanse of glazing. Achieving this with structural glazing is considerably more specialist and bespoke than using standard mullions.

The below images is a good example of the corner joint being a glass to glass silicone seal join, and to the middle of the face of the window you can see a standard aluminium mullion. I have attached a further image showing the sliding doors with structurally glazed fixed screens above. This gives a good indication of how the structural glass joins align with the slim interlocks of the sliding doors.

￼￼

The reason we specified this approach is because the original enquiry highlighted a preference for the fixed glazing to visually match the sliding doors. The sliding door system features a 20mm interlock, and by structurally bonding the glazing panels together, the silicone joint is approximately 10mm wide. We have then allowed for 20mm aluminium glazing bars over these joints, creating a sightline that closely matches the sliding door interlocks. These additional glazing bars are also a contributing factor to the overall cost.

The glazing specification is another important difference. Our quotation allows for 6.8mm laminated/ 6mm toughened glass throughout. The comparison quotation appears to allow for 4mm/4mm toughened and 6mm/6mm toughened units. As this is a New Build property, the glazing package will need to comply with PAS 24 requirements. For this reason, we have specified laminated/toughened glazing throughout and would always adopt this specification when using structural glazing. Across the quantity of glazing involved, this difference has a noticeable impact on the overall glass cost.

This was one of the areas I was particularly keen to clarify. I will send through an updated offer shortly based on the standard mullions, if you feel that standard aluminium mullions would be acceptable within the fixed glazing areas.

I also noticed a difference in the sliding door specification. We have allowed for PAS 24-compliant locking, which requires the handle to be positioned on the central interlock. This configuration is necessary to achieve PAS 24 accreditation. An additional benefit is that there are no handles located at the outer jambs, resulting in a cleaner and more minimal appearance, as the sliding sash disappears fully into the frame without visible hardware at the sides.

From the comparison quotation, it appears they have allowed for the standard handle arrangement with handles positioned at the side, although their quotation does note that the Cor Vision system can be supplied to PAS 24 approved.

I also noted that their quotation confirms Anthracite Grey, Black and White Gloss as their standard colour options, which is as expected.

Overall, my impression is that the other company operates as a more traditional glazing installer, whereas our expertise is heavily focused on minimal-frame aluminium systems and structural glazing. As a result, some of these finer details may not have been identified or included within their quotation simply because they are less commonly involved in this type of specialist work.

I completely appreciate that price is an important factor. However, on a project of this nature, which is clearly a high-end property, I believe it is equally important to consider the finer specification details, aesthetics and technical execution, rather than focusing solely on the lowest price.

I will get the updated comparison quote over shortly. We would then be more than happy to arrange a video call so that we can talk through the differences in more detail and see whether we can agree a way forward.', 'W8Yud3jAteIl9yoFpDap', '2026-06-17 12:45:01+00', 't', 'Deposit & Sales Contract', '1.8480972337962963', 'Contractor', '2026-09-25 10:48:35.805515+00', 'misha_full_run'),
